A||||17||fiero56||rotor resurfacing?||||5||
Z||000000||fiero56||07-12-2000||08:28 PM||||1988 Formula.<P>My new master cylinder has arrived, and my front calipers and new pads should arrive any day. Should I have my rotors resurfaced before installing the new pads. They are scored a little.<P>Next question. I haven't tried very hard yet, but the front rotors don't seem to want to come off. Are they just stuck on there good, or what. The rears just pull right off. (1988 brakes are the same front and rear; pads are the same, rotors are the same, calipers are different because of the parking brake).||||reg||5
Z||000001||Old Lar||07-12-2000||09:10 PM||||If you spent bucks on new pads,calipers and master cylinder, get the rotors resurfaced.  Unless they are thin, then its new rotors.  You can't spend too much to be able to stop your Fiero.<P>As far as getting the front rotors off, should come off like the rears I would think.  They are probably rusted on.||||reg||3
Z||000002||batboy||07-12-2000||09:12 PM||||Yes, you should have the rotors turned. You should also remove the hubs that contain the wheel bearings. You have to pop the hub dust cover off, remover the cotter pin, and unscrew the large hub nut. Repack the bearings, inner and outer, with high temp grease.<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 07-13-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||batboy||07-12-2000||09:14 PM||||Oops!<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 07-13-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||lawrence||07-12-2000||09:20 PM||||The rotors should come off.  Get a rubber or plastic mallet and gently tap on them.<P>I like nice flat rotors, but you can get away with some small grooves.  How scored are they?  If they are just a little grooved due to normal wear, then you can leave them alone.  However, if the grooves are .015" or deeper, you may want to get them turned.  When you do, go to a place that will just touch them to clean them up.  Most places take a sixteenth off regardless how bad they are.  After that, you may not have enough for a second cut.<P>Good luck,<P>-L<BR> <A HREF="http://members.nccw.net/webe" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nccw.net/webe</A>  - WebElectric Magazine||||reg||1
Z||000005||Phil||07-12-2000||09:58 PM||||Bat<BR>He has an 88 the front rotors are hats just like on real cars.||||reg||1
Z||000006||tgowens||07-12-2000||10:42 PM||||the hub and rotor is one complete assembly containing the bearings as batboy said - this is an '88 - the prior years used a separate rotor which fit over the hub.<P>Personally, i wouldn't install new pads without turning the rotors........<P>------------------<BR>'87 Fiero GT<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.tgowens.com/t_g_owens.gif">      <BR><A HREF="http://www.tgowens.com/fiero" TARGET=_blank>www.tgowens.com/fiero</A>||||reg||1
Z||000007||fiero56||07-12-2000||11:11 PM||||Just making sure you guys understand what the '88 setup looks like. Go to <A HREF="http://www.fierostore.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fierostore.com</A> <P>Then go to online catalog, then brakes, and then click rotors. You will see the '88 front/rear brake rotors.<P><BR>Here is a picture:<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88rotor.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Raydar||07-12-2000||11:16 PM||||56...<BR>I did the same thing on my Formula, a few months ago. Just tap the front of the rotor, around the edges, with a hammer. <I>Don't</I> hit it hard, or you'll ding (or break) the rotor. Just hard enough to set up a vibration to break the rust. A few light taps, and the rotor will almost fall off. <BR>I had to replace one of my front hub/bearing assemblies, too. That was a little slice of hell. Getting the three screws out was the easy part. It was rusted (or grunged) into the knuckle. Had to break out the BMFH, and drive it out from the rear.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><BR>If there are ridges in the rotor that are deep enough to hook a fingernail in, then I would have them turned. <P>------------------<P>Raydar<BR>88 Formula T-Top <BR>88 Coupe (ISO 3800)<P>"Some mornings, it just isn't worth<BR>chewing through the restraints."<p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 07-12-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||fiero56||07-12-2000||11:22 PM||||Then its agreed. The front rotors should come right off, just like the rears. Rust should be the only thing holding them on.<P>The front end is getting work done right now(will be replacing all four ball joints, all bushings will be poly, new calipers, new pads, stainless steel brake hoses, new master cylinder). Then the back end will get attention(calipers, pads, poly, brake hoses,). I can't wait until I get even marginal braking ability again.||||reg||1
Z||000010||EdsB52||07-13-2000||01:04 AM||||What bugs me is that machine shops don't turn rotors correctly. I was like everyone else and assumed turning a rotor on a lathe was just fine, until I talked to this wise old man who worked at the GM Proving Grounds in Mesa, Arizona. He said the only way to do it is with a mill, not a lathe. I guess it's neccessary if a rotor is warped, but if not, just install semi-metalic pads and go on. The pads will break any glaze, so you're just wasting rotor thickness by turning if you do it incorrectly.||||reg||1
Z||000011||batboy||07-13-2000||07:30 AM||||Never mind...didn't realize he had an 88...even though the post plainly said so.||||reg||1
Z||000012||TOM||07-13-2000||10:34 AM||||A mill? I am not to sure about that.||||reg||1
Z||000013||ChadMan||07-13-2000||12:49 PM||||Drew, my rotors looked good and weren't warped, so I didn't have them turned when I installed my new brakes.  Plus, I'm with EdsB52 - I didn't want to shorten the life of the rotors.  My brakes are MUCH better than before, and they were good to begin with - I'm sure you'll be really happy when you get done.<P>This is cool - when you get done we'll have to have a 60-0 stopping contest!  (Preferably in the street, right in front of 87ECO5's house.)||||reg||1
Z||000014||fiero56||07-13-2000||01:06 PM||||I'm just being cheap right now, or I would replace the rotors too. With all the car shows coming up(food, lodging, etc.) and all my projects, I can put $100 to better use. The rotors are not very thin, but they do have quite of bit of use on them.  I never realized I had such horrible brakes until I drove William T. III's '87 coupe, I couldn't believe how much better his brakes were than mine. Right now, I can stand on the pedal, and the nose will barely dive. Even in the rain I can't lock up any of the wheels. I think I have carbon metallics(or some type of lifetime pads)on the car right now from the previous owner. Hopefully the new calipers and pads will arrive. Then I will be able to lock the front tires. Along with stainless steel hoses, my brakes will be better than from the factory. <P>I have seen William attempt to perform a 60-0 stop in front of Scott's house. He locked up all 4 wheels.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Alex4mula||07-13-2000||01:25 PM||||It is very wise to check the rotor thickness before & after resurface. If they get thin you will get squeaking sounds when braking. If it is not your original car I would check this to be in the safe side. I had that problem in my Formula and got rid of it with new rotors.||||reg||1
Z||000016||87ECO5||07-13-2000||03:05 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B>This is cool - when you get done we'll have to have a 60-0 stopping contest!  (Preferably in the street, right in front of 87ECO5's house.)</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Well, at least <I>SOMEONE</I> will be slowing down!      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"><P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B>I have seen William attempt to perform a 60-0 stop in front of Scott's house. He locked up all 4 wheels.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I was pretty impressed.  The neighbors were not!    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by 87ECO5 (edited 07-13-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000017||theogre||07-13-2000||04:02 PM||||The rule is always turn a rotor when repalcing pads. They are nearly always warped or abraded and that will chew the new pads pretty quick.<P>The current "rules" for turning rotors call for a "non directional finish." You can actually see that finish in the picture of the rotor someone posted above. All new rotors come this way now. (And yes I imagine they are using a large mill to get that finish.)<P>When you turn rotor, or drums, on a lathe you get a spiral finish. This spiral tends to want to pull the pads/shoes and can actually make the pads skip over the surface. (It's kind of the same effect as wheel hop.) This reduces braking untill the spiral wears off. Depending on who did the cutting that spiral can be pretty bad and take considerable time to wear off.<P>The new finish method eliminates that spiral and the new brake pads seat almost instantly as a result. You have full braking effectivness from the first couple times you hit the pedal.<P>You can still turn rotors on a lathe. Getting the improved finish takes some effort.<P>First once the rotors have had been trued and rechecked for thickness, then you set the lathe to cut a fine as it posibly can and take a single pass cutting as little metal as posible. You're trying only to cut off the rough trueing pass's ridges here. <P>The rotor is now as flat as a lathe can make it. move the cutters away from the rotor.<P>With the rotor still mounted and spinning in the lathe chuck take a random orbit sander with 120-220 sand paper and lightly hold it against the spinnning rotor. Hold the sander as flat on the rotor as posible. You want as much sanding disk against the rotor as you can get.<P>Let the sander and lathe work until the spiral is polished off the rotor. You don't need to press hard on the sander. Use a fresh sanding disk for each side of the rotor. pay attention when doing this. It goes pretty quick if the lathe makes a good fine cut. <P>This won't give you a brand new rotor but it will allow you to use existing brake lathes and still get a good finish on the rotor.<P>It has also been sugested to me that you can do this with 3M gasket remover pads on a drill. That will likely work but seems alot more expencive way to do it. I would not try it with just a straight sanding disk on a drill. Too easy to dig a hole that way.<P>------------------<BR>Lake Superior? Is that what I stepped in? Sorry.||||reg||1
